Here's a few nifty ideas.


1. If you sacrifice a tin opener, your god should be able to accept
tinned corpses, assuming they were tinned while fresh. If you
sacrifice a tin before a tin opener, you could have a -1 alignment
penalty and abuse your wisdom ("Even <your god>'s powers can not open
the tin!"). The tin would have a time out of 100 turns* 50 -
turns-until-tinned, and then it would become unsuitable for
sacrificing ("This tin is passed it's use-by date!"). If you
sacrificed a tin of spinach,  you would get a +3 allignment bonus
("<your god> seems stronger!") If the tin was fresh meat your god
would accept the sacrifice, but with only 1/2 the normal gains to
allignment, luck etc. After each succesful sacrifice there would be 1
in 20 chance of needed to sacrifice a new tin opener ("You hear a
strange snapping and a muttered curse").

2. If you're polymorphed into a small creature, you should be able to
swim into fountains using the #jump command. ("You jump into the
fountain.")  Each turn you would have a 1/3 chance of usual fountain
effects ("You accidently swallow some of the water!"), 1/3 chance of
nothing happening ("You swim in the clear water") and a 1/3 chance of
equal chances of:
 1: Tiring yourself, abusiving your strength, losing 20 nutritain, and
a 1/2 chance of dropping a small portion of your gold ("Your muscles
tire of swimming") ("Some gold slips from your hands!").
2: Being refreshed by the swim, gaining 10 nutrition and excercising
strength ("You feel refreshed!").
3: Finding a corpse of another small monster and abusing wisdom unles
chaotic  ("You are disgusted as a <monster> corpse float by") ("You
grin evily as a <monster> corpse floats by.
4: If lawful, feeling blessed for 50 nutrition, gaining 1-10 HP (1/3
chance of max HP increasing by 1 if full health already) and excercise
all stats, but only once per fountain ("A hand reaches up from the
deep and blesses you!"), If chaotic losing 50 nutrition, losing 1-10
HP (but not below 1) and abusing all stats, but only once per fountain
("A freezing mist rising from the water and envelopes you!"),  if
neutral no effect ("You have a strange feeling for a moment, then it
passes").
5: Feeling the vapors of a random potion ("You see a <potion colour>
mist in the fountain"), then usual vapor effects.
6: An electric eel appears unless genocided, and you suffer an adition
d20 electrical damage each turn while still in fountain ("You brush
against an eel!")
7: Creating 1-3 squares of water on adjascent squares ("You splash
some water around!")
If you un-polymorph, or polymorph into a larger creature while in the
fountain it will break ("The fountain breaks into shards with your
increasing bulk!") and becomes a water tile, unless your new form can
go through the fountain (eg. Xorn). If you can swim normally or can
breathe underwater, you have a 1/4 chance of finding 20-50 gold, but
only once per fountain. If the fountain was magical, your HP and PW
are restored to full and maximum increased by 1, excercise all stats,
and have a 1 in 7 chance of each item being blessed, then make the
fountain normal ("Wow! That water feels great!"). While in fountain
suffer normal water effects (rust, etc.)

3. A new artifact: Lightbringer. Lawful broad-sword, crowning gift for
lawful healers or priests. Auto-moves into enemies (opposite effect of
stormbringer),  -2 to hit, -2 damage. Wielded: hungerless
regeneration, healing spells cost 50% less to cast (rounded up), acts
as a permenent light source for four squares. Invoked: Acts as a
potion of healing if cursed, extra healing if uncursed or full healing
if blessed to all monsters with four squares, but without extra
maximum HP like a normal potion.

4. New conducts: Preservetive, someone who leaves the dungeon in the
same state as it was when they entered. Namely, you can not change any
dungeon features or traps (except for the ones needed to win, eg. the
incovation). This includes accidently changing something, like
stepping on a land mine and forming a pit. Other monster can change
the dungeons without breaking the conduct. Items do not count (except
for traps).
Friendly: A character that never angers or kills, or let's another
monster or pet anger or kill a peaceful creature. They also can not
attack sleeping, paralised or fleeing monsters.
Single-minded: A character that never un-wields their original weapon
or takes off their original armour. (Easy for tourists)
Collecter: A character that keeps a tin of every monster that leaves a
corpse and can be tinned (Does anyone know how many tins would be
required for this?).
Destructionist: A character who kills every character they come across
in normal sight. This conduct is broken by leaving the dungeon level
without killing every monster you have seen directly. (except
Izchak!).
Magically unprotected: At no point in the game are you allowed to have
magic cancellation, reflection or magic resistance (except for one
turn as a wizard to take your cloak off)


